<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Undavalli Caves</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Undavalli Caves are rock-cut <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> cave temples carved into a sandstone hill on the south bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayawada">Vijayawada</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Generally attributed to the period of the Vishnukundina kings in the 5th to 7th centuries, the largest is a four-storeyed cave containing a huge reclining image of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> carved from a single block of rock.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/undavalli-caves.jpg" alt="Undavalli Caves" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Laxmanvety · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Undavalli_Caves,_Vijayawada,_Guntur,_Andhra_Pradesh,_India_(2018)_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
